// Tutorial schedule #2: split — two workers, one cross-worker edge.
//
// Same algorithm as naive.sched.nuc, decomposed across two workers:
//   host -- runs the effectful I/O kernels (owns the file system).
//   w0   -- runs the pure `scale_bias` kernel (the loop body).
//
// Every data symbol that crosses the host<->w0 boundary needs exactly
// one `transfer` directive (PRD §6.3.4: omitting a required transfer
// is a HARD error, not a silent default). Three symbols cross:
//   a (host -> w0)  produced by load_a     on host, read by scale_bias on w0
//   b (host -> w0)  produced by load_b     on host, read by scale_bias on w0
//   c (w0 -> host)  produced by scale_bias on w0,   read by save_c     on host
//
// All three are `sync`: the producer blocks until the consumer has
// received — the minimum semantic every tier-1 backend offers.
//
// The tutorial runs this on the `mp-tcp-bufsync` backend (OS processes
// over TCP loopback). The output MUST be byte-identical to the naive /
// pthreads-sync run above: that byte-identity across two radically
// different decompositions is the project's headline correctness gate.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0 };

    // I/O on host (owns the file system).
    place load_a on host;
    place load_b on host;
    place save_c on host;

    // Compute on w0.
    place scale_bias on w0;

    // One transfer per data symbol that crosses a worker boundary.
    transfer a : sync;
    transfer b : sync;
    transfer c : sync;
}
